WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Film Courage:

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:04.150
What the biggest success you feel you both
have had so far and where were you in your

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:05.780
life when it happened?

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.580
Elaine Zicree: Go ahead…

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:14.800
Marc Zicree: Okay…well for me the biggest
success I’ve ever had is meeting you, you

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:15.800
know?

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:16.800
That made everything.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.800
Everything that is good has come into my life
because of that.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:18.800
Elaine Zicree: Thank you.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.460
Marc Zicree: So that is the high point, you
know?

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:27.539
I mean, I’ve been very, very lucky because
I met Elaine when I was…I think I had just

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:28.880
turned 20.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.830
And so that was perfection.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:34.331
That was great because (again) when she met
me I was just a little college student, there

00:00:34.331 --> 00:00:38.460
was no telling if I would accomplish anything
and she had faith in me.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.520
So when I met her, I already knew she was
wonderful.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.180
But she was taking a big risk on me and it
worked out.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:51.500
But I’ve been very lucky because think of
all the people who as kids wanted to be writers

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.250
or directors or producers.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.040
They watch TV shows and they say “Gosh,
I’d like to be part of that world.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.320
I want to be on the other side of that glass
of the TV screen.”

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:01.320
And I got to do that.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.320
I got to be that.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.119
I mean, my heroes were Rod Serling and Ray
Bradbury because they were doing TV and books

00:01:07.119 --> 00:01:08.610
and movies.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:13.529
And I got to do all of that and so I am very,
very lucky.

00:01:13.529 --> 00:01:15.179
But Elaine had faith in me.

00:01:15.179 --> 00:01:19.009
And it’s funny because I was going to say
I was uninformed.

00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:23.880
When she met me, I wasn’t able to do any
of the things I can now.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.749
I mean…but she had already directed Off-Broadway,
she’d already been an actress, she’d had

00:01:28.749 --> 00:01:33.259
adventures and she had already proven her
value.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:37.280
Elaine Zicree: I think for both of us our
biggest successes are whatever we’re involved

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.280
in at the moment.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.329
We’re not looking back.

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:43.299
We’re sort of drunk on the moment, the enthusiasm
of the moment.

00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:46.270
And in a way, it’s kind of sad to say “Well
that was the big time.”

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:50.600
I think it’s the ongoing journey.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.939
The success is the ongoing journey and you
have to be a little bit crazy, you know, with

00:01:54.939 --> 00:01:55.950
each new thing.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.849
And say “This is going to be amazing.”

00:01:57.849 --> 00:02:00.100
And so I don’t think it’s one thing.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.450
Marc Zicree: We don’t rest on our laurels.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:03.789
Right now is the exciting time.

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:07.659
I mean to have my own studio, to walk into
a building filled with spaceship sets…I

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:10.810
mentioned that when I was 10, I went to the
Star Trek set and when I stepped onto that

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:14.750
stage with our spaceships, that’s the exact
same feeling, it’s the same thrill.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:16.920
I’m 10 years-old again.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.370
And to be able to create something it’s
like, every day is like Christmas.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:25.069
It’s just like basically my gosh, wonderful
actors, wonderful people to work with, and

00:02:25.069 --> 00:02:29.250
to be able to write, direct, and produce with
Elaine is such a thrill because she brings

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:33.680
the meaning, she makes sure that what I am
doing is meaningful and is working.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.990
And when we are done with this [video interview]
we will go back with our editor and sit down

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.599
and continue working.

00:02:38.599 --> 00:02:40.819
And that’s just thrilling.

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:44.280
And so, we are very lucky to be where we are
and do what we do.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.920
Film Courage: What would you say to someone
who might be listening to this who feels that

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:55.069
maybe they hit their peak (however many years
ago) and they feel there is no way up for

00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:56.319
them, for whatever reason?

00:02:56.319 --> 00:03:00.719
Elaine Zicree: I would say that this has nothing
to do with their craft.

00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:05.810
I think it has to do with feeling stuck in
their life and to take a walk around the block,

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:10.310
talk to people, volunteer, bring yourself
back to your work.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:11.310
Something has happened.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:15.060
There has been a disconnect and that I doubt
very much it has to do with their craft.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:22.219
It has to do with they have lost something
inside, which can easily be regained if you

00:03:22.219 --> 00:03:24.499
don’t tell yourself a story.

00:03:24.499 --> 00:03:26.379
You know, it’s the stories that destroy
us.

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:31.010
And to just say get re-enthused about your
life.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.889
And it’s not in the past.

00:03:32.889 --> 00:03:39.739
All of those years, even those years of misery
there’s…you know we were talking to a

00:03:39.739 --> 00:03:42.469
large group of writers in a seminar.

00:03:42.469 --> 00:03:49.050
And two of the writers in the far front corner
of this large room, they raised their hand

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:54.630
and said “Well we’re from Denmark and
we’d love to work here but we are very different,

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.090
we’re a different culture.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:59.700
We really feel like we have nothing to say,
we feel like such outsiders.”

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:03.639
And I said “Well why don’t you just turn
around and look at everybody else in the audience.”

00:04:03.639 --> 00:04:04.639
And they did.

00:04:04.639 --> 00:04:09.389
And I said “Okay, everybody else in the
audience, who here feels like an outsider?”

00:04:09.389 --> 00:04:11.739
And every hand in the room went up.

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:15.060
And I said “I think you’ve got something
to write about.”

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:19.509
What’s important that a lot of people don’t
realize is that “Oh, I feel so alone.”

00:04:19.509 --> 00:04:24.700
And you just say “Well, everyone tends to
feel secretly very alone.”

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:28.099
So you understand that even in your loneliness,
you’re not alone.

00:04:28.099 --> 00:04:30.889
So have the courage and share it.

00:04:30.889 --> 00:04:35.110
And that makes you and everybody else feel
less so.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:41.340
I think those feelings to say “I’m feeling
it but I know it’s bogus and I will push

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.340
through.”

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:46.990
Marc Zicree: I would also say in that regard
is that every day that you wake up is another

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:48.250
beginning.

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:51.380
And the past in a way is an illusion.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:55.250
It’s sort of like it’s gone, you can’t
reclaim it.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.610
So all you can do is say “What do I want
to do with this day?

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.460
What do I want to do with this moment?”

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:05.390
And you can be an entirely reborn person but
look for people who are positive.

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:07.680
Look for people who have a good life energy.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.050
Look for people who allow you your dream.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:13.220
And if you don’t find them where you are,
come here.

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:22.930
Come to where your dreams can be made possible
and don’t be stopped, don’t ever be stopped.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:23.930
Find the way to make it happen.

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:29.039
Elaine Zicree: In one of my scripts that I’m
in the middle of polishing, there’s a place

00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:30.820
of second chances.

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:36.300
And somebody says they don’t believe in
a place where you can get second chances and

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.930
they say “Well if somebody found out that
there’s a place of second chances then this

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:41.550
place would be flooded.”

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:46.310
And the fellow he is talking to laughs and
says “No…no…no, you’re thinking of

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:49.570
the town down the way of damn good excuses!”

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:54.830
And I think that when sometimes people feel
defeated part of them is looking for the good

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:58.370
excuse and not really for the second chance
because that we can make endlessly.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:04.620
Marc Zicree: Well one thing I will say in
conclusion in that regard, I would urge anyone

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:12.099
who has a dream to pursue it and to not be
deterred and to take a chance, you know?

00:06:12.099 --> 00:06:13.879
And to really go for it.

00:06:13.879 --> 00:06:20.229
And one day I came home and there was a message
on my answering machine and it was Ray Bradbury

00:06:20.229 --> 00:06:22.370
and he’d come across something I’d written
and he loved it.

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:24.990
And he invited me to his house.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:26.849
That was (of course) a thrill of a life time.

00:06:26.849 --> 00:06:30.650
And I went to his house and we hit it off
and we became great friends.

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:35.250
And the thing that amazed me about Ray was
that I knew him in his 80’s and into his

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:38.680
90’s, he died at 91.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:45.069
And through that whole time, I met him after
he’d had a stroke and his schedule as a

00:06:45.069 --> 00:06:50.500
writer had been to get up, 5 days a week,
write for three hours, then have lunch, take

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:56.039
a nap, do his business (the business of his
career in the afternoon) and then evenings

00:06:56.039 --> 00:06:57.560
and weekends were for his family.

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:00.699
And he did that for 70 years.

00:07:00.699 --> 00:07:05.919
And when I knew him, he’d had a stroke and
his mind was fine but he couldn’t type anymore

00:07:05.919 --> 00:07:07.970
and his handwriting was even very difficult.

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:12.199
So what he would do to continue writing was
he would wake up in the morning holding the

00:07:12.199 --> 00:07:17.919
words in his head, he would then call his
daughter in Arizona and dictate the words

00:07:17.919 --> 00:07:23.830
to her, she would type them and then fax them
to him and he would make hand corrections

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:27.220
on the typed script.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:28.630
That was how he wrote.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:33.129
And he was writing two new books a year through
his 80’s and into his 90’s.

00:07:33.129 --> 00:07:36.569
And he was made of iron.

00:07:36.569 --> 00:07:40.360
This was the kind of person that I wanted
to be my inspiration and my mentor because

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:43.169
I saw how determined he was.

00:07:43.169 --> 00:07:47.919
Nothing was going to stop him and that was
wonderful to see and certainly something I

00:07:47.919 --> 00:07:49.110
want to emulate.

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:51.889
I would urge everyone to take that to heart
and not be deterred.

